+### Layerwise Query selection per request
+
+On the Qwen3.5-core layerwise strategy, Chat Completions, Responses, Messages
+and text completions accept `kvmem.reselect`: `auto` (the default), `force`,
+or `off`. Optional `kvmem.query_begin` and `kvmem.query_end` describe the
+original half-open token range in the complete prepared prompt, including
+chat framing. Omitting `query_end` uses the complete prompt end. A manual
+range uses `auto` unless `force` is explicitly requested.
+
+`auto` skips a repeated Query only when its original range matches the
+range sealed with the selected native checkpoint, the checkpoint covers its
+end, and its token, position and media prefix is exact. A matching cache key
+or text prefix alone cannot skip it. `force` preserves the complete current
+Query and chooses or reconstructs a checkpoint before its aligned evaluation
+begin. It requires a trusted current Query; no cached old question is borrowed.
+`off` disables Query selection for this request and forbids a manual range.
+Ordinary tool continuation cannot recall with a cached old question.
+
+When an exact continuation has no effective Query and its actual resident
+rows, remaining prompt and output reserve fit with the required block
+headroom, it retains the selected views (`keep_selected`). Otherwise the
+ordinary Pressure schedule applies. When a request executes a new Query,
+selection can use the full configured historical KV budget, independently of
+the requested output limit. During generation, accepted rows fill the remaining
+physical window and trigger resident Pressure at block boundaries. The logical
+context and output limits still bound generation. The complete Query and fixed
+bands must fit at the selected checkpoint before its owner is restored or
+consumed. An invalid range or capacity failure is a request error.
+
+Nondefault Query policy requires enabled `--kvmem-recall-strategy layerwise`
+with a positive selected-token budget.
+The C++ Engine equivalent is `PreparedPrompt::set_layerwise_request_policy`
+with `KvmemRequestPolicy` from `ninfer/layerwise_request.h`; it keeps the
+prepared prompt's original tokens and tokenizer provenance.
+
+### KVMem statistics extension
+
+For experimental single-GPU Qwen prefill, `NINFER_PREFILL_PAGE_LSE_REUSE=1`
+records the already-computed softmax tile weights for the existing sampled
+queries. On the D256, 24-query-head / 4-KV-head, plain FP8 cache prompt route
+used on Ada, adjacent tiles contribute to each 64-token logical page. Each head
+is normalized over the whole visible cache before averaging. Query selection,
+Mean-K granularity, KV budget and sampling cadence are unchanged. The capture
+uses the existing feedback scratch. Decode, Query replay, incompatible shapes,
+short-row routes and unsupported devices use the existing computation. Leave the
+variable unset for the reference page-statistics route. The prompt kernel rounds
+its rotated Q and decoded K to BF16: its page probabilities need not be bitwise
+equal to the reference FP32 dot-product statistics, especially near a ranking tie.
+
+For long single-sequence Ada prefill, set `NINFER_PREFILL_FP8_WIDE_KV=1`
+together with `NINFER_PREFILL_PAGE_LSE_REUSE=1`. Each layer widens its
+visible K and V once into temporary BF16 and FP16 planes shared by its query
+blocks. The 64-row register tile retains the existing two 32-key softmax and
+PV updates per feedback page. The persistent cache remains FP8. Workspace is
+4 KiB per visible token rounded up to a 64-token page, shared across layers
+(160 MiB for 40,960 tokens). Widths below 128 or visibility above 65,536 use
+the existing prompt route. Both settings are read at planning and execution;
+set them before starting the server.
+
+KVMem's Qwen3.5-core dense path supports explicit heads tensor parallelism:
+`--devices 0,1 --tensor-parallel --tensor-attention heads`. Merely selecting two
+devices retains layer-pipeline placement. Optional `--tensor-split 3,1` weights
+follow device order; omitting them uses the upstream bandwidth/capacity planner,
+including KVMem's resident window and Query statistics. Actual GDN/KV heads and
+FFN columns are rounded to the model's legal stored-block boundaries. `/props`
+reports these ranges in `execution.tensor_shares`, alongside `tensor_split` and
+`tensor_attention`; requested ratios need not equal the realized head counts.
+
+Query capture and scoring retain global head order; attention feedback weights
+each rank by its number of query heads. RAM/SSD payload and recurrent-state
+identities include the actual partition. A cache with a different partition is
+incompatible. KVMem rejects `--tensor-attention positions` before loading;
+the native path without KVMem retains that upstream option. Ordinary generation,
+MTP and DFlash2 share this placement contract. A package's validation report
+identifies which models, weight/KV formats and hardware combinations were tested.
+
+KVMem builds preserve the native `usage`, `timings`, streaming usage selection, and
+`/metrics` names and meanings. Completed Chat Completions and Responses also carry
+`kvmem.speculative`, separately from those native fields. For Chat streams it is
+on the terminal timing chunk; for Responses it is in the terminal response body.
+
+The extension exposes native proposed/accepted draft counts, verification and
+fallback counts, accepted counts by draft position, and the Engine's actual
+`generation_rounds`. `output_tokens` excludes the first output token;
+`timing_scope: "first_to_last_token"` identifies the matching `wall_ms` interval.
+`tokens_per_round` and `ms_per_round` divide those totals by the observed round
+count. Without observed rounds they are `null`, with `unavailable_reason`, rather
+than being inferred from output tokens. Acceptance is accepted draft tokens divided
+by proposed draft tokens; the bonus token is excluded. Native MTP, ngram and DFlash
+counters keep their existing meanings.
